---
description: Use design tokens from the lookup table when building UI from Figma
alwaysApply: true
---

When building or editing UI from Figma or Framelink data, take colors, spacing, corner radius, border thickness, and fonts from @docs/token-lookup.md. Do not hardcode those values. The token export changes as screens are built. After `tokens/tokens.json` is replaced, run `npm run tokens` before matching values.

Copy the **Use this in code** cell. Prefer a semantic token (`mapped`) over an alias, and an alias over a brand primitive. If several tokens share a value, choose by role and use the Figma layer name as a hint: text → a `mapped.text` token, fills → a `mapped.surface` token, strokes → a `mapped.border` token, icons → a `mapped.icon` token. For a raw color, the lookup shows a CSS variable; in Tailwind use `bg-`, `text-`, or `border-` plus that same name, matching the role.

If no value matches exactly, use the nearest token and add `/* TODO token mismatch: Figma <value> */` beside it. List every mismatch in the reply.

Shadows, backdrop blur, and the dot grid stay as they are. Do not invent tokens for them, and do not flag them as hardcoded or as mismatches. Responsive type (font size, line height, paragraph spacing) is not generated yet, so keep those sizes as given and do not mark them as mismatches.

When a component, toolbar, dropdown, or popover is built or changed from Figma, its section in `docs/design-system-notes.md` has to stand on its own. This applies to the contextual button, the text toolbar, the text align popover, and every component built after them. Before the work is called done, that section contains:

- The Figma name, node id, ticket, and code path
- A token table for every piece: height, padding, gap, radius, stroke, fill, text color and icon color for each state, and the divider when the frame has one
- Its own table. A popover or any other component does not say that it matches the toolbar. Write that component’s values in its own table
- The Figma properties it exposes, with their values. Record state, type, selected, and any slot that can be turned off, when the component has those properties
- The states that are painted, and the states that are not

Shadow, blur, the dot grid, and type size are left out of the token build. In the table, mark them `not a token`. Do not call them a mismatch.

Each item in “Still to update” is tagged Figma, token app, or code. Move an item to “Decisions already made” once it is settled. Mention the new notes in the reply, under the component’s name.

```tsx
<div className="rounded-alias-border-radius-lg bg-mapped-surface-page p-brand-scale-400 text-mapped-text-default" />
```
